#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116417 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the values of $x$ that satisfy *either* $4x+2 < 6$ *or* $2x \\geq 8.$\n\nTo do this, we solve each inequality individually and then compute the union of the two solutions.\n\nWe start by solving $4x+2 < 6\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & <6 \\\\ 4x & <4 \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d36b8e68d9fd0b33b255502860481eeb525b60382029b17434bfb072cc39da2.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2x \\geq 8\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & \\ge 8 \\\\ x & \\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ed/edf5665b7cab7f51e3f252601a96d43c6f0cbfc64e5c9b8da81f0ef09d7b2f5a.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/5c/5c60839a626f2ba8a0fe4a24f4011af0fa52c00d79fbf80039bcfdbdf0a612c7.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $x < 1$ or $x \\geq 4.$ We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 1) \\cup [4,\\infty).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6d186c13-c1df-52d0-8ed6-a383185138a9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1428 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
